WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:09.680
When we think of viruses, we often think of the&nbsp;
biological agents responsible for Influenza,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:16.400
Ebola, or the COVID-19 Pandemic. The damage&nbsp;
caused by these viruses on society is significant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:21.680
But why do viruses infect us? What role do&nbsp;
they have in biology? And is there a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:27.680
we can harness viruses to benefit humanity?
Let’s start by looking at what a virus is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:35.760
Viruses are small infectious agents made up of&nbsp;
genetic material -- either DNA or RNA --surrounded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:42.080
by a protective protein layer called the capsid.&nbsp;
The capsid is made up of identical building blocks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:48.160
known as capsomers that fit together to make&nbsp;
the 3D structure of the virus. These can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:54.880
classified into non-enveloped viruses, that can&nbsp;
have a complex, helical, or icosahedral figure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:01.120
and enveloped viruses, which are spherical,&nbsp;
as the capsid is surrounded by a membrane.&nbsp;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:08.080
Unlike most other biological agents, viruses&nbsp;
can't reproduce on their own. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.600
they infect a host cell, hijack the cell's&nbsp;
machinery, and force it to produce new viruses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:21.680
The type of virus determines how it gets&nbsp;
inside a cell. Some non-enveloped viruses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:26.000
have proteins on their surface that bind&nbsp;
to receptors on the outside of cells.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:31.680
Think of the viral surface protein as a key&nbsp;
that fits into a lock on the cell’s surface.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:36.400
If the protein fits, the cell is&nbsp;
unlocked and the virus can enter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:43.040
Other non-enveloped viruses attach to&nbsp;
the outside of the cell and essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.440
poke a needle through the membrane to&nbsp;
deposit viral genetic material inside.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:53.840
Enveloped viruses, on the other hand, are&nbsp;
surrounded by a membrane like the one on cells.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:59.120
This allows the virus to fuse with&nbsp;
the cell, releasing the capsid inside.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.240
Regardless of the strategy&nbsp;
viruses use to enter the cell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:10.080
the process that follows is the same. The viral&nbsp;
genetic material ends up at the ribosomes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.960
the cellular machinery that will “translate”&nbsp;
the viral genetic material into proteins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:21.120
These come together to form new viruses,&nbsp;
which can leave the cell, either by pushing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.840
through the membrane or by lysing it,&nbsp;
which can eventually lead to cell death.&nbsp;

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:32.320
The human body has natural defenses against&nbsp;
viruses, such as immune cells that attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:38.640
them and antibodies that bind to them. After&nbsp;
a virus is successfully eliminated, the immune&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:45.120
system retains a memory of the pathogen. This&nbsp;
way, the next time the same virus infects us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:50.960
our bodies can respond faster and more&nbsp;
efficiently. Unfortunately, our bodies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:56.960
are not always capable of eliminating the virus.&nbsp;
In this case, the best way to protect ourselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:03.040
is through vaccines, a form of artificial immunity&nbsp;
that involves administering low doses of weakened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:09.280
viruses to the body. This mimics the process of&nbsp;
natural immunity, allowing the body to generate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:14.720
a memory of the virus without the vaccinated&nbsp;
individual actually developing the disease.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:21.600
When no vaccine or treatment exists and the body&nbsp;
cannot mount a sufficient attack on its own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:26.880
we are at risk of the virus replicating out of&nbsp;
control and spreading throughout the population.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:36.000
A prime example of this is the COVID-19 pandemic,&nbsp;
caused by the novel coronavirus SARS-CoV-2. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.120
virus is thought to be zoogenic—meaning it&nbsp;
was first transmitted from animals to humans.&nbsp;

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:47.440
The first outbreak was recorded in&nbsp;
Wuhan, China in December of 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:52.480
and the virus quickly spread across the&nbsp;
globe, infecting over 20 million people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.480 --> 00:04:00.720
and killing at least 736,766, as of August 2020.
Despite all the harm that viruses can cause,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:06.240
most of them don’t actually infect humans.&nbsp;
In fact, some are critical to our survival.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:14.080
Bacteriophages, for instance, are complex viruses&nbsp;
that help us fight off harmful bacteria. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:18.400
hijack the bacteria that are infecting the&nbsp;
body and force them to halt the production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:23.200
of harmful bacterial components, stopping&nbsp;
the damage that the bacteria were causing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:29.280
Since bacteriophages do not damage our cells&nbsp;
in the process, they have been used to fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:36.800
bacterial infections like E.coli and staph. As&nbsp;
bacteria become more resistant to antibiotics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:43.600
antibiotics can fail in protecting us, which is&nbsp;
where bacteriophages can become especially useful.&nbsp;

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:50.640
With new technology, we are learning how to&nbsp;
harness viruses to use them for our benefit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:56.960
Nowadays we treat some as messenger vessels,&nbsp;
by removing the potentially dangerous genes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:01.680
viruses would normally insert to our&nbsp;
DNA and replacing them with sequences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:08.480
that can be useful for us. For example, the&nbsp;
herpes virus has been used to treat cancer, by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:14.480
replacing the genetic material of the virus with&nbsp;
new DNA that can target and attack tumor cells.&nbsp;

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:18.880
Although there is still a lot&nbsp;
to be learned about viruses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:24.000
it is becoming clear that they can be more&nbsp;
than just the diseases they are known to cause.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:37.840
They might even become the&nbsp;
next superheroes of medicine.

